values
  ('flying-when-you-are-frightened-of-it', 'Flying When You Are Frightened of It', 'The dread starts weeks before and peaks at the gate. What the fear is actually made of, what the noises mean, and how to get through a flight you have already booked.', 'The holiday is booked and you are already dreading it. Not the trip, the three hours in the air. You check the weather for the route. You read about the aircraft type. You lie awake the night before, and at the gate you look at the plane through the glass and think about turning around.

Then every bump in the air becomes evidence, and you spend the flight gripping an armrest and watching the cabin crew''s faces for a sign.

What the fear is usually made of

Fear of flying is rarely one fear. It helps to work out which pieces are yours, because they need different handling.

Fear of a crash, which is a fear about the aircraft.

Fear of panicking, of being trapped, of being unable to get off or get help, which is really a fear of your own body and of confinement rather than of flying.

Fear of heights, of turbulence, of not being in control, of being judged for being frightened.

Most people who think they are afraid of crashing are substantially afraid of the second one. That matters, because fear of panicking responds to panic treatment, which is highly effective, and not to statistics about aviation.

Get the facts about the noises

A great deal of in-flight fear is misread information. Knowing what the ordinary sounds are removes a lot of it, because you stop reading routine events as warnings.

Turbulence is uncomfortable air, not damage. Aircraft are built to flex and the wings are designed to move. Cabin crew sit down because carrying hot drinks is unpleasant in it, not because something is wrong.

The thumps and whines after takeoff are the wheels retracting and the flaps coming in. The engine note dropping a few minutes after takeoff is a normal power reduction. The bump on landing is intentional firmness.

If the specifics help you, airline-run fear of flying courses go through all of it with a pilot in the room and they are among the most useful things available.

Stop the pre-flight rituals that feed it

The checking is not preparation, it is reassurance-seeking, and reassurance wears off faster each time.

So: no reading about accidents, no checking the weather for the route, no tracking the aircraft''s history, no watching takeoff videos. Each of those gives twenty minutes of calm and strengthens the idea that you need information to be safe.

Alcohol is also worth skipping. It makes sleep worse, dehydrates you, and makes the panic response more likely later in the flight.

Have a plan for your body, not for the airplane

You cannot control the aircraft. You can control what your body does with the adrenaline, and that is where the work pays off.

Breathe out longer than you breathe in, which lowers your heart rate when nothing else will. Press your feet into the floor and your back into the seat. Hold something cold. Keep your hands busy with something that needs a little attention: a puzzle, a game, a book you can actually follow.

Air vents on your face help a lot of people, as does a cold drink and taking a layer off, because heat makes panic worse.

Let the wave crest rather than fighting it

Panic peaks and falls, on a plane exactly as on the ground. The thing that makes it last is fighting it and watching for it.

So when it starts, name it: this is panic, it is extremely unpleasant, it is not dangerous, it will peak and pass. Then keep doing whatever you were doing, badly, rather than stopping everything to manage the feeling.

Tell the cabin crew

This one surprises people. Telling a flight attendant quietly that you are a nervous flyer gets you noticed in the right way: a check-in, a word about the turbulence, somewhere to look. They deal with this on most flights and nobody thinks anything of it.

Practical choices that genuinely help

A seat over the wing, which moves least. An aisle seat if being hemmed in is the problem, a window if looking out helps you. A morning flight, since afternoon air is bumpier in warm weather. A direct flight rather than two takeoffs and two landings. Someone with you who knows and will not tease you.

Be careful with medication as the whole plan. Something prescribed for one flight can help you get on the plane, and relying on it every time tends to leave the fear intact, because you never find out you could do it. Worth a conversation with your doctor rather than a borrowed pill.

Go, if you can

Fear of flying shrinks lives quietly: jobs not taken, weddings missed, family not visited. The thing that reliably reduces it is flying, several times, while using the tools, and letting your nervous system find out what actually happens.

If avoidance has gone on for years, do not expect to resolve that alone the week before a wedding. Get help in advance.

When to get help

Please talk to your doctor or a therapist if fear of flying is shaping what you do with your life, or if the fear is really about panic, confinement or losing control, since those have specific and effective treatments. Cognitive behavioral therapy for panic and phobias works well and does not take years. Airline courses help many people, and a pilot explaining turbulence does more than any amount of private research. If the dread has left you hopeless or you have had thoughts of not wanting to be here, please tell someone today. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Panic', 'Thinking Traps']::text[], 0, '2026-10-27T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('the-month-you-move', 'The Month You Move', 'Everything has to be sorted, packed, canceled and re-registered, and all of it lands at once. How to get through the month of a move without losing your temper or your sleep.', 'There is the move itself, which is one day, and then there is the month around it, which is where the damage happens. Boxes in the hall. A hundred small decisions about things you have not looked at in nine years. Utilities, schools, addresses, deposits, a van, keys, and somebody asking you a question while you are holding a lamp.

And you cannot explain to anybody why you are close to tears about a kitchen drawer.

Why moving is harder than its parts

A move is an unusually dense pile of small decisions, and decisions are the expensive part. Should this be kept, where does it go, who do I tell, when. Hundreds of them, most of them trivial, all of them needing you.

Underneath that there is often a loss nobody acknowledges. Even a move you chose means leaving a place where things happened. If the move came with something harder, a separation, a death, a job ending, money, then you are grieving and doing logistics at the same time, which is why the smallest thing can tip you over.

Put everything in one place, out of your head

The most useful hour you can spend is writing one list of everything the move requires, then giving each item a week.

Address changes: bank, doctor, dentist, insurance, work, school, licenses, subscriptions, anything that bills you.

Services: electricity, gas, water, internet, with the dates.

Logistics: the van or the movers, keys, parking, elevator booking, where the cat goes, who has the children that day.

Money: deposits, final readings, overlapping rent.

One list is less frightening than the same list scattered across your mind at three in the morning.

Pack an open-first box, and pack it first

Not last. The thing that makes the first night bearable is one clearly marked box you can find immediately.

Kettle, mugs, tea, toilet paper, soap, towels, phone chargers, a change of clothes, bedding, any medication, scissors, a bulb, and whatever makes the place feel like yours. Everything else can wait.

Make the bed before you do anything else in the new place. People who do this consistently report the first night as fine, and people who do not are assembling a bed frame at eleven at night in a bad mood.

Do not sort while you pack

This is the single biggest time sink. Opening a box of photographs or old letters while packing turns a twenty-minute job into a three-hour afternoon that leaves you sad and behind.

Pack the obvious, bin the obvious, and put anything requiring a decision into clearly labeled boxes to sort after the move. Keep one box for sentimental things and give it its own evening, later, with a drink and no deadline.

Guard the last week

The last week before a move is where sleep, food and patience go. Protect three things.

Sleep, by stopping at a set time even with boxes unfinished. The boxes will still be there and you will be faster tomorrow.

Food, by giving up on cooking entirely for a few days without guilt.

One small ordinary thing a day: a walk, a shower, ten minutes outside. It will feel like the thing you do not have time for and it is the thing keeping you reasonable.

Expect to be short with the people you love

Nearly everyone who moves with a partner has a bad argument in the final week, usually about something absurd. It is not a sign about the relationship. It is two tired people making hundreds of decisions in a house full of boxes.

It helps to say so in advance: this month is going to make us both snappy, and when it happens let us notice it rather than taking it as evidence. Divide territory rather than deciding everything together, since most of these fights are about the overhead of joint decisions.

Say goodbye to the old place on purpose

Walk through the empty rooms before you hand the keys over. Take a photograph of the room that mattered. Say thank you if you are the sort of person who would.

This sounds sentimental and it does a job: it marks the ending, which makes the new place feel less like limbo.

Then expect the strange weeks afterward

Arriving is not the end. Most people have a stretch of feeling flat, displaced, or quietly homesick, even in a better house, and are surprised by it because the hard part is supposed to be over.

It settles faster if you unpack the kitchen and the bed first, get out and walk the local streets, find one place you can go regularly, and let the rest of the boxes sit for a while.

When to get help

Please talk to your doctor if the stress of a move has turned into not sleeping for weeks, chest pain, headaches or a mood that has not lifted once you are in. If the move came with a loss, a separation or a job ending, that is grief as well as upheaval and a counselor is worth it rather than waiting to feel better. And if money is the part keeping you awake, a free debt or housing adviser will do more good than any amount of coping on your own.', array['Stress', 'Life Transitions']::text[], 0, '2026-10-27T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-you-are-sure-you-are-a-burden', 'When You Are Sure You Are a Burden', 'The thought that people would be better off without you feels like a plain fact. Where that belief comes from, why it is so convincing, and what to do with it tonight.', 'It does not arrive as a feeling. It arrives as a conclusion, already settled: I am a drain on the people around me, they are being polite, their lives would be easier without me in them.

And because it feels like a fact rather than a mood, you do not argue with it. You act on it. You cancel things, you stop asking, you reply later and shorter, you take up less room.

Where the belief comes from

Almost nobody arrives at this out of nowhere. It usually has a source, and naming the source loosens it a little.

Low mood produces it directly. Depression does not only lower how you feel, it alters what seems true, and one of the most common distortions is reading yourself as a burden. When mood lifts, that thought goes quiet, which tells you something about where it came from.

Illness, pain or disability can bring it, especially where you need practical help.

Being told it, by someone who was angry or struggling or cruel, once or for years.

Growing up having to be no trouble, where needing things was unwelcome.

Being genuinely in a hard stretch, needing more than usual, and having nothing to give back yet.

Why it is so convincing

It has the shape of evidence. You can list the things people do for you, the times you canceled, the money, the lifts, the conversations about your problems. The list is real.

What is missing is everything else: what you give that you do not count, what people actually feel about helping you, and the plain fact that needing help is a condition every human passes through rather than a defect in you.

Depression also removes your ability to see yourself from outside. It is genuinely hard to notice that you are being read by the people around you as a person they love and not as a cost.

Do not act on it tonight

This is the most useful rule. The belief wants action: withdraw, cancel, apologize, go quiet, take up less space. Each of those makes it stronger tomorrow, because distance confirms what it told you.

So treat it as a thought to be carried rather than obeyed. I am having the burden thought again. I am not going to make any decisions about my relationships while it is this loud.

Check it, rather than argue with it

Arguing with yourself about your worth is unwinnable. What can be checked is the prediction.

Write down what you believe will happen: if I ask my sister for a lift, she will sigh and I will see it. Then ask, and write down what actually happened.

Try asking for something small on purpose, once a week, specifically to gather evidence. Most people find the answers are warm and unremarkable, and that unremarkableness is the most convincing thing available.

Let people answer for themselves

You are currently making a decision on behalf of other people, about how much they mind, without asking them.

So ask one person, plainly: I have been worried I am too much for you lately. They will usually be surprised, and sometimes hurt that you thought it. If there is something, you will hear a version you can actually work with, which is far better than the version you invented.

Notice what you do give

Keep a note for two weeks of things you did for other people, however small: a message, listening, a lift, remembering something, being funny, being there. This list is the one you never make, and it is as real as the other one.

If you are in a stretch of needing more than you give, that is a stretch rather than an identity. Relationships are settled over years, not weeks, and almost everyone has years where they take more.

Reciprocity is not a ledger

The belief treats closeness as an account that has to balance. That is not how it works for anybody. People help the people they love and do not run the arithmetic, and when they are the ones struggling, they will want the same from you.

Being needed by someone you love is not a cost. Most people would rather be asked than protected from it.

The dangerous version of this thought

Sometimes this does not stop at I am too much. It becomes they would be better off without me, or a sense that your absence would be a relief.

That is not a conclusion to sit with alone, and it is not the truth. It is one of the clearest signals that the illness is doing the thinking.

If you are there, please tell somebody today: your doctor, an urgent service, a person in your house. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.

When to get help

Please talk to your doctor or a therapist if this belief is around most days, if it has lasted more than a couple of weeks, or if it is making you pull away from people. It is one of the most recognizable features of low mood, it responds well to treatment, and a therapist can help you test it rather than just arguing with it. If it began after an illness or alongside needing care, say that too, since there is practical support that lowers how much you have to ask of the people close to you.', array['Depression', 'Self-Compassion']::text[], 0, '2026-10-27T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('after-you-ate-more-than-you-meant-to', 'After You Ate More Than You Meant To', 'The eating is over and the hard part has started: the self-attack and the plan to make up for it. What actually helps in the next hour, and how to stop the cycle restarting.', 'It is done. You are sitting there feeling uncomfortable and slightly unreal, and the voice has already started: what is wrong with you, you have no control, you have ruined it.

Then comes the plan. Nothing tomorrow. Skip breakfast. Extra exercise. Start again Monday and be strict this time.

That plan is the problem, and it is worth understanding why before you act on it.

The restriction is usually what set it up

For most people this is a cycle rather than an event, and it runs in a predictable order.

You decide to be strict. You eat too little, or skip meals, or cut out whole categories. Your body gets genuinely hungry and your mind gets preoccupied with food. Eventually that pressure breaks, usually in the evening, usually fast and not very enjoyably. Then shame arrives, and shame produces a stricter plan, which builds the next one.

So the resolution to make up for it tomorrow is not the cure. It is the first step of the next round.

Do the next hour differently

The hour after is where the cycle is either fed or broken, and almost nothing you do with food in that hour matters as much as what you do with the self-attack.

Stop eating and do not purge, over-exercise or fast to compensate. Those make the physical discomfort worse and strengthen the pattern.

Make yourself physically comfortable. Looser clothes, a glass of water, a short slow walk if that feels all right, somewhere to sit that is not where it happened.

Then let the discomfort pass without commentary. It does pass, usually within a couple of hours, and it is not an emergency.

Say something to yourself that is both kind and true

Not it is fine, because you do not believe that. Something accurate.

That was more than I wanted. I was hungry and tired and had a bad day. Eating too much in one evening does not undo anything. My next meal is normal.

That last sentence is the whole of it. The single most useful thing is to eat your next ordinary meal at the ordinary time.

Eat normally tomorrow, deliberately

This feels backward and it is what breaks the cycle. Breakfast, lunch, dinner, something in between if you are hungry.

Skipping meals after a big night guarantees the same evening again within a few days, because you arrive at six o''clock underfed and the pressure is back. Regular eating is the single most effective change people make here, and it works better than any amount of resolve.

Find out what it was actually about

Food is sometimes about food, and often about something else. In the next day or two, when you can think, ask what was going on beforehand.

Were you underfed that day. Over-tired. Did something happen: a conversation, a rejection, a dull lonely evening, a feeling you did not want to be in.

Most people find two or three reliable triggers. That list is worth more than any rule about what you eat, because it tells you what the eating is doing and what else might do part of the same job.

Watch for the all-or-nothing word

The thinking that drives this is nearly always absolute. I have ruined it. I have no willpower. I might as well finish it now and start again Monday.

That is the thought that turns a single evening into a week. The accurate version: one evening of eating more than I planned, in a month where most evenings were fine.

If you can catch it mid-way, the same applies. Stopping after more than you meant to is a better outcome than deciding the day is lost, and it is allowed.

Take the moral weight out of it

Food is not virtue. You were not good last Tuesday and bad last night. The language of being good with food is where a lot of this pain comes from, and dropping it makes the whole subject quieter.

Notice the signs this is bigger than a habit

Some things point to an eating difficulty that deserves real help rather than another plan.

Eating large amounts with a sense of being out of control, regularly. Making yourself sick, using laxatives, or exercising to cancel food. Eating in secret and hiding it. Food and your body taking up most of your thinking. Fasting for long stretches. Feeling that your worth depends on what you eat or what you weigh.

Those are common, they are not a character problem, and they respond well to treatment. People wait years before mentioning them, usually out of shame, and almost everyone says afterward that saying it out loud was the hardest part.

When to get help

Please talk to your doctor if this happens regularly, if you are compensating afterward in any way, or if food has become the loudest thing in your head. Ask specifically about eating disorder services or a dietitian who works with this, and say the plain truth rather than the tidier version, since what is available often depends on it. A therapist can help with the shame and the all-or-nothing thinking that keeps the cycle turning, and that part is as treatable as the eating itself.', array['Food', 'DBT']::text[], 0, '2026-10-27T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('the-argument-you-keep-having', 'The Argument You Keep Having', 'Same subject, same lines, same ending, over and over. Why some arguments never resolve, how to find what the fight is really about, and what to do when it starts again.', 'You could perform both parts. It starts with the dishes, or the money, or their mother, or being late. Within four minutes you are both saying the sentences you always say, and within ten it has become about something enormous, and then it ends the way it always ends: someone leaves the room, or someone gives in, and nothing is different.

Then three weeks later it happens again, from the beginning.

Some arguments are not about their subject

A fight that resolves is usually about a problem. A fight that repeats is usually about a difference, and differences do not resolve, they get managed.

Underneath most repeating arguments is a pair of things each person is protecting. Not positions, values. Being reliable. Being free. Being respected. Being safe about money. Being taken seriously. Not being controlled.

The dishes are real and the dishes are not why it goes nuclear. It goes nuclear because one of you hears I am not considered and the other hears I am being managed, and those are not solved by a chore rota.

Find out what each of you is actually protecting

This is a calm-weather conversation, not a mid-argument one.

Each of you answers, separately and in writing if that helps: when this comes up, what am I afraid of. What would it mean about me, or about us, if I gave in completely.

Then swap. The answers are usually more sympathetic than either of you expected, and considerably less about the dishes.

Notice your part of the loop

Repeating arguments are a machine with two operators. Yours has a move in it: going cold, getting loud, bringing up history, bringing up something they said in April, going quiet, over-explaining, getting the last word, leaving the room.

You cannot change theirs. Changing yours changes the shape of the thing immediately, because the loop needs both halves to run.

Stop the first ninety seconds from setting the pattern

Most of these arguments are decided in the opening. Always, never, you are the one who, here we go again, and you are in the familiar groove within a sentence.

So change the opening. One specific event, one feeling, one request, no history.

The dishes were there when I got in last night and I felt like the only one who notices. Can we work out something that does not rely on me asking.

It is less satisfying and it does not invite the fight.

Agree on a way to pause it, in advance

Mid-argument, nobody can invent a sensible exit. Agree on one beforehand: a word, a hand signal, twenty minutes, and a commitment to come back rather than disappear.

The coming back is the part people skip, and it is what makes a pause different from stonewalling.

Separate the two conversations

Repeating arguments mix up what happened and what it means, and mixing them means neither gets settled.

So do them separately. The practical one: who does what, by when. The other one, later and in better weather: what this subject means to each of us. Trying to do both at once at eleven at night is why it has gone around eleven times.

Aim for workable rather than agreed

A great many of these never end in agreement, and do not need to. Partners who do well with a long-running difference usually land on an arrangement rather than a resolution: he handles this, she handles that, we do it differently and neither is wrong, we accept that one of us will always care more about this.

Writing the arrangement down sounds unromantic and prevents a surprising amount of repetition, because most relapses start with two different memories of what you agreed.

Ask whether it is a difference or a problem

Worth being honest here. Some repeating arguments are a difference in temperament, and the work is acceptance on both sides.

Others are a genuine imbalance: one person doing nearly all the work, one person spending money the other does not have, one person''s family given a say the other never agreed to. Those are not solved by better communication. They need the actual imbalance to change, and endless calm conversations about them can become a way of not changing anything.

And some are not an argument at all. If the pattern includes contempt, threats, intimidation, being punished for days, or being frightened of raising things, that is something different and it deserves outside help rather than a better script.

When to get help

If the same argument has been running for years, or you have stopped raising things because of where it goes, couples counseling is worth it, and most people wish they had gone sooner. Going alone helps too if your partner will not: a therapist can work on your half of the loop, which changes the whole of it more than you would expect. Please talk to your doctor if the conflict is affecting your sleep or your mood, and if you are ever frightened of your partner, tell a professional that plainly rather than treating it as a communication problem.', array['Anger', 'Values']::text[], 0, '2026-10-27T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz)
on conflict (slug) do update set
  title        = excluded.title,
